<page>
<title>True / False</title>
<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            (8 points) Suppose <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and <m>\vec b\in \mathbb R ^{m}</m> unless otherwise stated. Select <em>true</em> if the statement is true for all choices of <m>A</m> and <m>\vec b</m>.  Otherwise, select <em>false</em>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="true">
    <statement>
        <p>
            <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are orthogonal <m>n \times n</m> matrices, then <m>UV</m> is also an othogonal <m>n \times n</m> matrix.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            True.
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text
        </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; Any set of vectors <m>\{ \vec{v_1},\vec{v_2}, \dots , \vec{v_n}\}</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> can be transformed into an orthogonal basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> using the Gram-Schmidt process.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                False.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>    
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">`
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>W</m> is an <m>m</m>-dimensional subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, then <m>\text{dim}(W^{\perp}) = n-m</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                True
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A^TA</m> is not invertible, then there is no least-squares solution to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                False
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\{ \vec{u_1}, \vec{u_2} \}</m> is a basis of the subspace <m>W</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, and <m>\vec{y_1}, \vec{y_2} \in W^{\perp}</m>, then <m>\vec{u_1} + \vec{y_1} \neq \vec{u_2} + \vec{y_2}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                True
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>P</m> is the <m>n \times n</m> matrix of the orthogonal projection onto a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, then <m>P^2 = P</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                True
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\vec{x} = \hat{x}</m> is the vecotr in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> which minimizes <m>|| A \vec{x} - \vec{b}|| </m>, then <m>\hat{x}</m> is a least-squares solution of <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                True
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>W</m> is a 1-dimensional subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> and <m>\text{proj}_W\vec{u} = \text{proj}_W\vec{v}</m>, then <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                False
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    
    
</problem>


<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           (4 points) Indicate whether the following situations are possible or impossible. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A <m>3 \times 3</m> orthogonal matrix <m>U</m> so that <m>\text{det}(U) = 2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                impossible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution Text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A square matrix <m>A</m> that is invertible but not diagonalizable.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                possible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution Text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A subspace <m>W</m> and a vector <m>\vec{y}</m> so that if <m>\hat{y} = \text{proj}_W(\vec{y})</m>, then <m>||\vec{y}|| = 5, ||\hat{y}|| = 4</m>, and <m>||\vec{y} - \hat{y}|| = 3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                possible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution Text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A <m>5 \times 5</m> matrix with real entries that haas no real eigenvalues.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                impossible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution Text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>




</problem>

<problem workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>        
            (2 points) Which of the following is true for every <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> with <m>m \lt n </m>?

            <m>\textit{Select only one}</m>
        </p>
        
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li> <m>(\text{Col}(A))^{\perp} = \text{Nul}(A)</m> </li>
            <li><m>\text{dim}(\text{Nul}(A)) = \text{dim}(\text{Nul}(A^T))</m></li>
            <li><m>\text{dim}(\text{Col}(A)) + \text{dim}(\text{Nul}(A^T)) = m</m></li>
            <li><m>(\text{Nul}(A))^{\perp} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m</m></li>
        </ol>

    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            Option (c) is correct
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text
        </p>
    </solution>

    </problem>



</page>


<page>


<p workspace="0.5in">
    
</p>

<problem workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            (6 points) Fill in the blanks.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> are orthogonal vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> and that <m>\vec{u}</m> is a unit vector. If <m>(2 \vec{u} + \vec{v}) \cdot (3 \vec{u} + 2 \vec{v}) = 14</m>, determine the length of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                ||\vec{v}|| = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>||\vec{v}|| = 2</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text
        </p>
    </solution>
    </problem>
    <problem workspace="true">

    <statement>
        <p>
            Compute the length of the vector <m>\vec{y}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                \vec{y} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{\sqrt{11}} \amp -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp \frac{3}{\sqrt{22}} \\ \frac{3}{\sqrt{11}} \amp 0 \amp - \frac{2}{\sqrt{22}} \\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{11}} \amp \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \amp \frac{3}{\sqrt{22}} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}
            </md>

            
        </p>

        <p>
            <md>
                ||\vec{y}|| = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>

     <answer>
        <p>
            <m>||\vec{y}|| = 3</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text
        </p>
    </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="true">

    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -2 \\ 4 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. The vector <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} i \\ 1+1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>. Find the associated eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> for the eigenvector <m>\vec{v}</m> of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <m>\textit{Note: You may leave your eigenvalue in the form } \frac{a+bi}{c+di}</m>
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                \lambda = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
            </md>
            
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\lambda = 1 + 2i</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text
        </p>
    </solution>
</problem>
    
</page>

<page>
<problem workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>        
            (3 points) Consider the equation <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> where <m>A = QR</m> is a <m>QR</m>-decomposition of <m>A</m> with 
            <md>
                Q = \frac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}, R = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}, \vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            </md>
            Compute the least squares solution to <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>

            <md>
                \hat{x} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
            </md>
        
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m> \hat{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text.
        </p>
    </solution>
</problem>

<problem workspace="3in">

    <statement>
        <p>
            (3 points) Let <m>W = \text{Span} \left\{ \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m>. Find a basis <m>\mathcal{B}</m> for <m>W^{\perp}</m>, the orthogonal complement of <m>W</m>.
        
            <md>
                \mathcal{B} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \\ f \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
            </md>
        </p>

    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <md>\mathcal{B} = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix},\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right\}</md>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text.
        </p>
    </solution>
</problem>

</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (5 points) Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\vec{y} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
                
            </p>
            <p>
                Find the orthogonal projection <m>\hat{y}</m> of <m>\vec{y}</m> to <m>W = \text{Col}(A)</m>.

                <md>
                    \hat{y} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \\ f \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \hat{y} = \frac{2}{3} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the orthogonal projection of <m>y^{\perp}</m> of <m>\vec{y}</m> to <m>W^{\perp}</m>.

                <md>
                    y^{\perp} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \\ f \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
            <p>
                <md>
                    y^{\perp} = \frac{1}{3} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text 
            </p>
        </solution>
        
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the distance from <m>\vec{y}</m> to <m>W</m>.

                <m>\textit{Hint: The distance between a vector } \vec{v} \textit{ and a subspace } W \textit{ is } ||\vec{v} - \vec{w}|| \textit{ where } \vec{w} \textit{ is the vector in } W \textit{ which is closest to } \vec{v}.</m>

                <md>
                    \text{distance}(\vec{y}, W) = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \\ f \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \text{distance}(\vec{y}, W) = \frac{\sqrt{6}}{3}
                </md>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text 
            </p>
        </solution>
        
    </problem>

</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>    
                (5 points) Show your work for part (a) on this page and put your answers in the boxes.
                
            </p>

            <p>
                Suppose that 
                <md>
                    \vec{x_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\  -1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}, \vec{x_2} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}, \vec{x_3} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.
                </md>
                (a) Find an orthogonal basis <m>\mathcal{B}</m> for the subspace <m>W = \text{Span} = \{\vec{x_1},\vec{x_2}, \vec{x_3} \}</m>

                <md>
                    \mathcal{B} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \\ f \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \mathcal{B} = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (b) Find the orthonormal basis <m>\mathcal{C}</m> for the subspace <m>W = \{\vec{x_1},\vec{x_2}, \vec{x_3} \}</m>

                <m>\textit{Hint: You may use your answer to part (a)}</m>

                <md>
                    \mathcal{C} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \\ f \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \mathcal{C} = \left\{ \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

